<!-- ================= Extreme Values and Saddle Points ================= -->
<worksheet xml:id="subsection-abs-extrema">
    <title> Exercises for <xref ref="section-extreme-values"  text="title"/></title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
      If you would like to review this topic before attempting the exercises,
      click <xref ref="section-extreme-values" text="title"/>.
    </p>
    </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-extrema-triangle">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
            <md>f(x,y)=x^2-xy+y^2-3y</md>
            on the region bounded by the <m>x</m>- and
            <m>y</m>-axes and the line <m>x+y=4</m>.
          </p>

          <interactive
            xml:id="hints-extrema-triangle"
            width="80%"
            aspect="5:4"
            iframe="iframe/hint-extrema-triangle.html"/>
        </statement>

         <solution>
      <p>
        The gradient is <m>\nabla f = \langle 2x - y,\; -x + 2y - 3\rangle</m>, which
        vanishes only at <m>(1,2)</m>, a point in the interior of the region. There
        <m>f(1,2) = -3</m>. Since this is the only critical point on all of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and it is a minimum (by the second derivative test), it is a
        global minimum. We now examine the three boundary segments to look for possible
        maxima.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>x</m>-axis with <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(x,0) = x^2</m>, which
        is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>y</m>-axis with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(0,y) = y^2 - 3y</m>,
        which is maximized at <m>f(0,4) = 4</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, on the line <m>x = 4 - y</m> with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have
        <m>f(4-y,\,y) = 3y^2 - 15y + 16</m>, which is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Altogether, there is an absolute minimum <m>f(1,2) = -3</m> and an absolute maximum
        <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
      </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-ex-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> has two local maxima, then it must have a local
minimum too.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
This is true for single-variable functions but no
longer holds in two dimensions. For example, the function
<m>f(x,y) = -x^4 + x^2 - \frac12 y^2</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-rm2-two-maxima"/>, has two local maxima at
<m>\left(\pm\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, 0\right)</m> with a saddle point at the
origin lying between them, and no local minimum.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rm2-two-maxima">
<caption>
The graph of <m>f(x,y) = -x^4 + x^2 - \frac12 y^2</m>: two local
maxima with a saddle point between them, and no local minimum.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional surface with two rounded peaks of equal height,
one on the left and one on the right, separated by a saddle shaped
dip in the middle. Away from the peaks the surface falls off in every
direction, so there is no lowest point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=10.5cm, height=7.5cm,
  view={-40}{28},
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none,
  clip=false,
]
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=gray!45, draw=gray!60,
          fill opacity=0.55, draw opacity=0.45,
          domain=-1.15:1.15, y domain=-1.05:1.05,
          samples=17, samples y=13]
  {-x^4 + x^2 - 0.5*y^2};
% the two maxima
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=purple!70!black]
  coordinates {(-0.7071,0,0.25) (0.7071,0,0.25)};
% the saddle point
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7, color=blue!60!black]
  coordinates {(0,0,0)};
\node[above, purple!70!black] at (axis cs:-0.7071,0,0.30) {max};
\node[above, purple!70!black] at (axis cs:0.7071,0,0.30) {max};
\node[above right, blue!60!black] at (axis cs:0,0,0.02) {saddle};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-ex-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find and classify all the critical points of
<m>f(x,y) = xy\,e^{x^2+y^2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
\nabla f(x,y) = \left\langle
\left(1 + 2x^2\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2},\;
x \left(1 + 2y^2\right) e^{x^2+y^2}
\right\rangle.
</md>
Since the exponential factors, as well as <m>1 + 2x^2</m> and
<m>1 + 2y^2</m>, are always positive, we might as well ignore them
when finding the zeros. So we are left with
<m>\langle x, y\rangle = \langle 0, 0\rangle</m>, i.e. the only
critical point is <m>(0,0)</m>. The second derivatives of <m>f</m> are
<md>
<mrow>f_{xx} \amp = \left(6x + 4x^3\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{xy} \amp = \left(1 + 2x^2\right)\left(1 + 2y^2\right)
e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{yy} \amp = x\left(6y + 4y^3\right) e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>D(0,0) = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = -1</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is
a saddle point.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>

